p.src:
For a genuinely complex example, prepare $\ket{x+}$ and measure $\sigma_2$. Then $\begin{aligned}
  \braket{y+}{x+}
  &=
  \frac12
  \begin{pmatrix}1&-i\end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}
  =\frac{1-i}{2},
  \notag\\
  P(\sigma_2=+1\mid\sigma_1=+1)
  &=\left|\frac{1-i}{2}\right|^2
  =\frac14+\frac14
  =\frac12.
  \end{aligned}$ Preparing spin along one axis and measuring along a perpendicular axis therefore gives equal probabilities for the two outcomes.

